  "query_preview": "..Northern Arizona is currently under the influence of\na short-wave ridge induced by a West Coast low centered off the \ncoast of north/central California. Water vapor satellite imagery \nshows that very dry low and mid-level air has overtaken the region.\nPreciptable Water (PW), a measure of atmospheric moisture, has \ndropped to between 0.25-0.50 inches. For comparison, PW values\nyesterday morning ranged from 0.35-0.75 inches. Model soundings \nindicate the atmosphere is too dry and stable for shower or \nthunderstorm development today, and the forecast calls for dry \nweather across the area. Otherwise, expect increasing high cloud \ncover associated with the West Coast low, light surface winds and \nnear-normal high temperatures. \n\nOn Monday, model forecasts are in good agreement that the West\nCoast low will begin to influence northern Arizona's weather as \nit moves inland across central California. High resolution model \nguidance has become increasingly confident that the area will see \na return of shower and thunderstorm development beginning as \nearly as Monday afternoon, especially in the northwest around \nGrand Canyon Country. The current forecast calls for a 30-50% \nchance for showers and thunderstorms in the northwest, with a \n10-25% chance along the Mogollon Rim and White Mountains.\n\nOn Tuesday, the low lifts to the northwest. As it does so,\nincreasing atmospheric moisture and forcing associated with the \nlow will bring more widespread shower and thunderstorm \ndevelopment, with the best potential for precipitation occurring \nfrom the Mogollon Rim northward. In addition, southerly wind \nspeeds are forecast to increase, but are expected to remain below \nWind Advisory thresholds, with gusts near 30 mph forecast over \nhigher terrain. According to the high-resolution model guidance, \nchances for rain rates exceeding 0.5 inches in 3 hours are \nminimal, indicating a low risk of flash flooding on Monday and \nTuesday.\n\nModel spread increases significantly beginning Wednesday, \nindicating decreasing confidence in the evolution of the longer- \nterm weather pattern late next week. This is due to uncertainty \nregarding interactions between a deep Gulf of Alaska low, the\npotential development of a closed low somewhere off the coast of \nnorthern Baja, and activity in the tropical eastern Pacific. It is\ndifficult to say exactly how these features will interact, so the\ncurrent forecast maintains chances for showers and thunderstorms \neach day through the remainder of the work week.",
